emaining work needed to complete the package for release 00.0. I need to pick between three options for the next task. Option 2 stands out—extending the format package with column alignment. It's focused and well-defined, the package already has established patterns to follow, multiple utilities depend on it, and it's part of the current release. Let me draft the task proposal.\n\nI'm checking the constraint about maximum requirements per task and reviewing the PRD anchoring requirement—the title needs to include the specific requirement ID like \"prd003-format R1\". I'm settling on \"prd003-format R1 -- Column alignment for tabular output\" as the title, though I realize I don't have visibility into the sub-requirement breakdown (R1.1, R1.2, etc.) from the source code alone, so I'll need to keep the scope description general enough to cover what R1 encompasses.\n\nEstimating the implementation scope at around 100-200 lines of Go code, which fits the target range. Now I'm drafting the task definition with the proper structure, including the deliverable type, required reading references to the PRD and existing package conventions, and setting up the stitch agent to handle the actual requirement details.\n\nBuilding the task YAML structure... I need to ensure all exported functions have requirement tracing comments following the established pattern, use only standard library dependencies, and meet the acceptance criteria of compiling successfully and producing correctly padded tabular output.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering the scope here—I should double-check the use case structure and whether I should reference the test suite in the required reading. The `cmd/version` command might be a simpler, more clearly scoped alternative that would complete the magefiles use case instead, since I can't fully verify all the PRD details for the column alignment task.\n\nBut `cmd/version` is probably too small at 20-50 lines when the constraint targets 150-250 lines per task, and 